<section class="panel" id="url-anatomy" aria-labelledby="url-anatomy-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Six parts, one string</p>
    <h2 id="url-anatomy-heading">Anatomy of a URL</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">A web address looks like one string, but it is six distinct parts, each with its own job. Hover or tab through them.</p>
    <div class="panel__body url-anatomy">
      <div class="url-part" style="--marker-color: var(--teal);">
        <span class="anatomy-marker">
          <button type="button" class="anatomy-marker__dot" aria-describedby="tip-protocol">1</button>
          <span class="anatomy-marker__tooltip" id="tip-protocol" role="tooltip"><strong>Protocol</strong>Tells the browser how to connect. HTTPS means the connection is encrypted, HTTP means it isn't.</span>
        </span>
        <code>https://</code>
      </div>
      <div class="url-part" style="--marker-color: var(--coral);">
        <span class="anatomy-marker">
          <button type="button" class="anatomy-marker__dot" aria-describedby="tip-subdomain">2</button>
          <span class="anatomy-marker__tooltip" id="tip-subdomain" role="tooltip"><strong>Subdomain</strong>A named section of the domain, like lab or www. It's optional. www is really just a subdomain by convention.</span>
        </span>
        <code>lab.</code>
      </div>
      <div class="url-part" style="--marker-color: var(--sun);">
        <span class="anatomy-marker">
          <button type="button" class="anatomy-marker__dot" aria-describedby="tip-domain">3</button>
          <span class="anatomy-marker__tooltip" id="tip-domain" role="tooltip"><strong>Domain</strong>The site's actual registered name. This is the one part a visitor needs to remember or trust.</span>
        </span>
        <code>webpageanatomy.com</code>
      </div>
      <div class="url-part" style="--marker-color: var(--pink);">
        <span class="anatomy-marker">
          <button type="button" class="anatomy-marker__dot" aria-describedby="tip-path">4</button>
          <span class="anatomy-marker__tooltip" id="tip-path" role="tooltip"><strong>Path</strong>The specific page being requested, like a folder path on the server. Each slash is a level deeper.</span>
        </span>
        <code>/dictionary/modal/</code>
      </div>
      <div class="url-part" style="--marker-color: var(--teal);">
        <span class="anatomy-marker">
          <button type="button" class="anatomy-marker__dot" aria-describedby="tip-query">5</button>
          <span class="anatomy-marker__tooltip" id="tip-query" role="tooltip"><strong>Query string</strong>Extra key-value data passed to the page, often used for tracking, filtering, or search terms.</span>
        </span>
        <code>?ref=homepage</code>
      </div>
      <div class="url-part" style="--marker-color: var(--coral);">
        <span class="anatomy-marker">
          <button type="button" class="anatomy-marker__dot" aria-describedby="tip-hash">6</button>
          <span class="anatomy-marker__tooltip" id="tip-hash" role="tooltip"><strong>Hash</strong>Jumps the browser to a specific spot on the same page. It never gets sent to the server at all.</span>
        </span>
        <code>#what-it-means</code>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" aria-labelledby="patterns-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reading patterns</p>
    <h2 id="patterns-heading">How Eyes Move</h2>
    <div class="panel__body card-grid">
      <div class="sticker coral">
        <span class="sticker__tag tag-chip">pattern</span>
        <h3>F-Pattern Reading</h3>
        <p>Users scan text-heavy pages in an F-shape: top-left to right, then down the left side. One of the more rigorously studied patterns in web UX, from Nielsen Norman Group's eye-tracking research.</p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary pattern-example-btn" id="openFPatternModal">See example</button>
      </div>
      <div class="sticker teal">
        <span class="sticker__tag tag-chip">pattern</span>
        <h3>Z-Pattern for Landing</h3>
        <p>On image-heavy, low-text pages, eyes tend to move in a Z: top-left, top-right, diagonal down, bottom-left to right. It's a looser heuristic than the F-pattern, more of a starting hypothesis than a law.</p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary pattern-example-btn" id="openZPatternModal">See example</button>
      </div>
      <div class="sticker pink">
        <span class="sticker__tag tag-chip">grid</span>
        <h3>The 8pt Grid System</h3>
        <p>All spacing uses multiples of 8px. It creates visual rhythm and makes responsive design predictable. It's the same convention behind Google's Material Design spacing scale.</p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary pattern-example-btn" id="openGridPatternModal">See example</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="sections" aria-labelledby="sections-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Building blocks</p>
    <h2 id="sections-heading">The Essential Sections</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Master these ten essentials and you'll understand most of web design.</p>
    <div class="panel__body card-grid card-grid--3">
      {% set colors = ["coral", "teal", "pink", "sun", "coral", "teal", "pink", "sun", "coral", "teal"] %}
      {% set realTags = ["<header>", "<nav>", "100vh", "<h1>–<h6>", "margin", "<footer>", "<section>", "4.5:1", "<button>", "768px"] %}
      {% for s in sections %}
      <div class="sticker {{ colors[loop.index0] }}">
        <span class="sticker__num">{{ s.number }}</span>
        <span class="sticker__tag tag-chip">{{ realTags[loop.index0] }}</span>
        <h3>{{ s.title }}</h3>
        <p>{{ s.description }}</p>
        <ul class="tips">
          {% for t in s.tips %}<li>{{ t }}</li>{% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="typography" aria-labelledby="type-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Type system</p>
    <h2 id="type-heading">Typography Scale</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Consistent type sizes create hierarchy and make content scannable. This scale is live. It's the same Fredoka/Nunito pairing driving every heading on this page.</p>
    <div class="panel__body sticker plain">
      <div class="type-scale-row"><span class="type-scale-row__label">H1 · 48–80px</span><span style="font-family:var(--font-display); font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3.5rem); font-weight:600;">Main Headlines</span></div>
      <div class="type-scale-row"><span class="type-scale-row__label">H2 · 32–48px</span><span style="font-family:var(--font-display); font-size:clamp(1.5rem,4vw,2.25rem); font-weight:600;">Section Headers</span></div>
      <div class="type-scale-row"><span class="type-scale-row__label">H3 · 24px</span><span style="font-family:var(--font-display); font-size:1.5rem; font-weight:600;">Subsection Headers</span></div>
      <div class="type-scale-row"><span class="type-scale-row__label">Body · 16–18px</span><span>Regular paragraph text for reading. Line height should be 1.5–1.7 for comfortable reading.</span></div>
      <div class="type-scale-row"><span class="type-scale-row__label">Caption · 12–14px</span><span class="mono">LABELS, METADATA, AND SMALL PRINT</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="color-system" aria-labelledby="color-system-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Design system</p>
    <h2 id="color-system-heading">Color as a System</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">This page uses six colors, total. Four bright sticker colors for accents, plus ink and paper for text and background. Everything else is a variation of those six.</p>
    <div class="panel__body color-system-demo">
      <div class="color-system-demo__row"><span class="color-system-demo__swatch" id="csPaperSwatch"></span><span>Paper (background)</span><code id="csPaperValue">#FFF9F2</code></div>
      <div class="color-system-demo__row"><span class="color-system-demo__swatch" id="csInkSwatch"></span><span>Ink (text)</span><code id="csInkValue">#22182B</code></div>
      <div class="color-system-demo__row"><span class="color-system-demo__swatch" id="csSurfaceSwatch"></span><span>Surface (cards)</span><code id="csSurfaceValue">#FFFFFF</code></div>
      <div class="color-system-demo__row"><span class="color-system-demo__swatch" id="csAccentSwatch"></span><span>Accent (links)</span><code id="csAccentValue">#B8175E</code></div>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary" id="colorSystemToggle" style="margin-top:1rem;">Toggle dark mode and watch these change</button>
    </div>
    <p class="panel__lede" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">These are not four separate palettes to maintain. They are CSS custom properties, and dark mode just redefines four of them. Every color on this page reads from these tokens instead of a hardcoded value, so the whole page repaints correctly from one attribute change.</p>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <p><strong>Semantic color:</strong> color should mean something consistently. On this page, teal always marks navigation-related things, sun yellow always marks a note worth pausing on, and a dashed border always means "editable" or "example," not just decoration.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="spacing" aria-labelledby="spacing-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">8pt grid</p>
    <h2 id="spacing-heading">Spacing System</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Consistent spacing creates visual rhythm. Every value below is a real multiple of 8, rendered at true size.</p>
    <div class="panel__body sticker plain">
      <div class="spacing-row">
        <div class="spacing-swatch"><div class="spacing-swatch__box" style="--box-size:8px;"></div><span class="spacing-swatch__label">8<br>Tight</span></div>
        <div class="spacing-swatch"><div class="spacing-swatch__box" style="--box-size:16px;"></div><span class="spacing-swatch__label">16<br>Default</span></div>
        <div class="spacing-swatch"><div class="spacing-swatch__box" style="--box-size:24px;"></div><span class="spacing-swatch__label">24<br>Relaxed</span></div>
        <div class="spacing-swatch"><div class="spacing-swatch__box" style="--box-size:32px;"></div><span class="spacing-swatch__label">32<br>Loose</span></div>
        <div class="spacing-swatch"><div class="spacing-swatch__box" style="--box-size:48px;"></div><span class="spacing-swatch__label">48<br>Section</span></div>
        <div class="spacing-swatch"><div class="spacing-swatch__box" style="--box-size:64px;"></div><span class="spacing-swatch__label">64<br>Major</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="panel__lede" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Boxes are one thing. Here's the same 8pt system applied to an actual card, next to a card that ignores it.</p>
    <div class="panel__body spacing-example">
      <div>
        <p class="spacing-example__label bad">✗ Ignores the grid</p>
        <div class="mini-card mini-card--bad">
          <div class="mini-card__icon"></div>
          <h3>Weekly Digest</h3>
          <p>Your top stories, curated every Monday morning.</p>
          <span class="btn btn--primary">Subscribe</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="spacing-example__label good">✓ Follows the 8pt grid</p>
        <div class="mini-card mini-card--good">
          <div class="mini-card__icon"></div>
          <h3>Weekly Digest</h3>
          <p>Your top stories, curated every Monday morning.</p>
          <span class="btn btn--primary">Subscribe</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> use 8px for inline elements, 16px for related items, 24–32px between groups, and 48–96px between major sections.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="buttons" aria-labelledby="buttons-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interaction design</p>
    <h2 id="buttons-heading">Button States</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Every button has at least four states, plus two more people tend to forget: focus, and whatever happens while it's working. These aren't illustrations. Hover, click, and tab to the buttons below to trigger each state for real.</p>
    <div class="panel__body button-section-grid">
      <div class="sticker plain">
        <div class="state-demo">
          <div class="state-demo__item">
            <span class="state-demo__label">Default / Hover / Active</span>
            <div class="state-demo__row">
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary">Try Me</button>
              <span class="state-demo__hint">Rest, hover, and click. Each one triggers a real CSS state, not a label.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="state-demo__item">
            <span class="state-demo__label">Focus</span>
            <div class="state-demo__row">
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary">Tab to Me</button>
              <span class="state-demo__hint">Tab to this button with your keyboard to see the focus ring. It's required for anyone not using a mouse.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="state-demo__item">
            <span class="state-demo__label">Disabled</span>
            <div class="state-demo__row">
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary" disabled>Disabled</button>
              <span class="state-demo__hint">Genuinely inert, using the native <code>disabled</code> attribute, not a style trick.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="state-demo__item">
            <span class="state-demo__label">Loading</span>
            <div class="state-demo__row">
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary state-demo__async" id="loadingDemoBtn" data-default-label="Submit">
                <span class="state-demo__async-label">Submit</span>
              </button>
              <span class="state-demo__hint">Click it. A real async action takes time, and a button that just sits there makes people click it twice.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="state-demo__item">
            <span class="state-demo__label">Success</span>
            <div class="state-demo__row">
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary state-demo__async" id="successDemoBtn" data-default-label="Save">
                <span class="state-demo__async-label">Save</span>
              </button>
              <span class="state-demo__hint">Confirming a completed action closes the loop. Without it, people re-click just to be sure it worked.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sticker plain">
        <h3>Button Styles Through Time</h3>
        <p style="margin-top:0.4rem;">States are one axis. Style trend is another. The same "click me" rectangle has gone through a lot of looks.</p>
        <div class="button-era-grid">
          <div class="button-era">
            <span class="button-era-stage"><span class="button-sample button-sample--skeuomorphic">Submit</span></span>
            <strong>Skeuomorphic</strong>
            <span class="mono">~2000s–2012</span>
            <p>Gradients, bevels, and drop shadows made buttons look like real, physical, pressable objects.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="button-era">
            <span class="button-era-stage"><span class="button-sample button-sample--flat">Submit</span></span>
            <strong>Flat Design</strong>
            <span class="mono">~2013–2016</span>
            <p>iOS 7 killed the gloss overnight. Solid color, no shadow, no gradient. Just shape and label.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="button-era">
            <span class="button-era-stage"><span class="button-sample button-sample--material">SUBMIT</span></span>
            <strong>Material Design</strong>
            <span class="mono">~2014–2019</span>
            <p>Google's answer to flat: keep it simple, but add a soft "elevation" shadow so it still reads as liftable.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="button-era">
            <span class="button-era-stage"><span class="button-sample button-sample--neumorphic">Submit</span></span>
            <strong>Neumorphism</strong>
            <span class="mono">~2020</span>
            <p>Soft UI: a button carved from the same surface as its background, using only light and shadow.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="button-era">
            <span class="button-era-stage"><span class="button-sample button-sample--brutalist">Submit</span></span>
            <strong>Neo-Brutalist</strong>
            <span class="mono">Now</span>
            <p>Thick borders, hard offset shadows, zero subtlety. This page's own buttons are a softer cousin of this style.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="button-era">
            <span class="button-era-stage"><span class="button-sample button-sample--pill">Submit</span></span>
            <strong>Pill / Rounded-Full</strong>
            <span class="mono">Now</span>
            <p>Fully rounded ends, minimal chrome. The default look of most modern SaaS and mobile apps today.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="forms" aria-labelledby="forms-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive states</p>
    <h2 id="forms-heading">Forms and Inputs</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">A form is where a visitor commits to something. Get the small details wrong here and people abandon it. Try the fields below.</p>
    <div class="panel__body card-grid card-grid--2">
      <div class="sticker teal">
        <h3>Label vs. Placeholder</h3>
        <p>A placeholder disappears the moment someone starts typing, so if it was the only clue about what a field wants, that clue is gone. A label stays visible the whole time.</p>
        <div class="form-demo-field">
          <label for="formDemoBad" class="sr-only">Email, placeholder only</label>
          <input type="text" id="formDemoBad" class="text-input" placeholder="Email address" />
          <span class="mono form-demo-hint">✗ Placeholder only. Start typing and the hint is gone.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="form-demo-field" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <label for="formDemoGood">Email address</label>
          <input type="text" id="formDemoGood" class="text-input" placeholder="you@example.com" />
          <span class="mono form-demo-hint">✓ Real label. Still there after you type.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sticker pink">
        <h3>Inline Validation</h3>
        <p>Real-time feedback beats a wall of errors after submit. Type an invalid email, then fix it, and watch the field respond as you go.</p>
        <div class="form-demo-field">
          <label for="formDemoValidate">Email address <span aria-hidden="true">*</span><span class="sr-only">, required</span></label>
          <input type="email" id="formDemoValidate" class="text-input" placeholder="you@example.com" required aria-describedby="formDemoValidateMsg" />
          <p id="formDemoValidateMsg" class="form-demo-message" role="status"></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p><strong>Input types matter:</strong> <span class="mono">type="email"</span> brings up an @ key on mobile. <span class="mono">type="tel"</span> brings up a number pad. Using <span class="mono">type="text"</span> for everything means a worse keyboard for every mobile visitor.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="zindex" aria-labelledby="zindex-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Stacking context</p>
    <h2 id="zindex-heading">Z-Index: The Layer Cake</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Z-index controls stacking order. It's not random. It's an org chart: modal at 1000, sticky nav at 100, dropdowns at 10, content at 1.</p>
    <div class="panel__body sticker plain">
      <div class="zindex-demo">
        <div class="zindex-layer zindex-layer--1">z-index: 1 · Base content layer</div>
        <div class="zindex-layer zindex-layer--2">z-index: 10 · Dropdowns &amp; tooltips</div>
        <div class="zindex-layer zindex-layer--3">z-index: 100 · Sticky navigation</div>
        <div class="zindex-layer zindex-layer--4">z-index: 1000 · Modals &amp; overlays</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="semantic" aria-labelledby="semantic-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Structure</p>
    <h2 id="semantic-heading">Semantic HTML: The Layer Underneath</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">These two mockups render identically. Same boxes, same text, same position on screen. One of them is invisible to a screen reader. Toggle the outline to see which.</p>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary" id="toggleSemanticOutline" aria-pressed="false">Show the outline</button>
      <div class="semantic-compare">
        <div class="semantic-mock">
          <p class="semantic-mock__code mono">div › div › div › div › div</p>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--header">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip" hidden>div</span>
            Logo &nbsp; Home &nbsp; About &nbsp; Contact
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--main">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip" hidden>div</span>
            <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--article">
              <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip" hidden>div</span>
              Article title and a paragraph of body text sit here.
            </div>
            <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--aside">
              <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip" hidden>div</span>
              Related links
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--footer">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip" hidden>div</span>
            &copy; Copyright
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-mock__outline" hidden>
            <strong>What a screen reader hears:</strong>
            <p>"div. div. div. div. div." No landmarks to jump between. No way to skip straight to the content.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="semantic-mock">
          <p class="semantic-mock__code mono">header › nav › main › article + aside › footer</p>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--header">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip semantic-outline-tag--header" hidden>header</span>
            Logo
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--nav">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip semantic-outline-tag--nav" hidden>nav</span>
            Home &nbsp; About &nbsp; Contact
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--main">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip semantic-outline-tag--main" hidden>main</span>
            <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--article">
              <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip semantic-outline-tag--article" hidden>article</span>
              Article title and a paragraph of body text sit here.
            </div>
            <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--aside">
              <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip semantic-outline-tag--aside" hidden>aside</span>
              Related links
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-block semantic-block--footer">
            <span class="semantic-outline-tag tag-chip semantic-outline-tag--footer" hidden>footer</span>
            &copy; Copyright
          </div>
          <div class="semantic-mock__outline" hidden>
            <strong>What a screen reader hears:</strong>
            <p>"Banner landmark. Navigation landmark. Main landmark. Complementary landmark. Content info landmark." Five jump points, for free.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="a11y-list" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">SEE</span>
          <p>Sighted users never notice the difference. Both mockups look pixel-identical. The gap is entirely in the layer underneath what you can see.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">HEAR</span>
          <p>Screen readers build a navigable outline from real landmark elements. A wall of <code>div</code>s gives them nothing to build it from.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">CRAWL</span>
          <p>Search engines and browser reader-modes lean on <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> to figure out what the actual content is, separate from nav and chrome.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="a11y" aria-labelledby="a11y-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Inclusive design</p>
    <h2 id="a11y-heading">Accessibility: Design for Humans</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Build for everyone. Not everyone uses a mouse. Not everyone can see your colors.</p>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <div class="a11y-list">
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">ALT</span>
          <p>Describe images for screen readers by their function, not just their contents. "Team celebrating a product launch" beats "IMG_4829.jpg."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">3:1 / 4.5:1</span>
          <p>WCAG AA needs <strong>4.5:1</strong> contrast for normal text, but only <strong>3:1</strong> for large text (18px+, or 14px+ bold) and UI component borders.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">TAB</span>
          <p>Tab through your site. If you can't reach something without a mouse, it's broken. Every marker and demo on this page is a real, focusable element.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">ARIA</span>
          <p>Use ARIA to help screen readers understand interactive elements that native HTML can't describe alone, but reach for semantic HTML first and ARIA second.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">FOCUS</span>
          <p>Every focusable element needs a visible focus indicator. It's easy to forget because a mouse never triggers it. See the Button States demo above.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <pre style="margin-top:1.25rem;"><code>&lt;button aria-label="Close modal"&gt;✕&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;img src="hero.jpg" alt="Team celebrating a product launch"&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="touch" aria-labelledby="touch-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Real-world usability</p>
    <h2 id="touch-heading">Touch and Mobile</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">More visitors are tapping with a thumb than clicking with a mouse. Design for that reality, not just for yourself at a desk.</p>
    <div class="panel__body card-grid card-grid--2">
      <div class="sticker sun">
        <h3>Tap Target Size</h3>
        <p>A touch target needs to be at least 44×44px to be reliably hit with a finger. Compare the two buttons below at their real, unscaled size.</p>
        <div class="tap-target-demo">
          <div class="tap-target-demo__item">
            <button type="button" class="tap-target tap-target--small" aria-label="24 pixels, too small">24</button>
            <span class="mono">✗ 24×24px</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tap-target-demo__item">
            <button type="button" class="tap-target tap-target--good" aria-label="44 pixels, correct size">44</button>
            <span class="mono">✓ 44×44px</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sticker coral">
        <h3>Thumb Zones</h3>
        <p>On a typical one-handed phone grip, the bottom of the screen is easy to reach, the middle takes a stretch, and the top corners are the hardest. Put primary actions low.</p>
        <div class="thumb-zone-demo" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="thumb-zone thumb-zone--hard">Hard to reach</div>
          <div class="thumb-zone thumb-zone--ok">Stretch</div>
          <div class="thumb-zone thumb-zone--easy">Easy</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p><strong>Hover doesn't exist on touch.</strong> The button below changes color when you hover it with a mouse. On a touchscreen there is no hovering, a tap either activates the element immediately or does nothing. Never hide required information behind a hover-only state.</p>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary hover-only-demo" style="margin-top:0.75rem;">Hover me (with a mouse)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="head-meta" aria-labelledby="head-meta-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">What the browser sees first</p>
    <h2 id="head-meta-heading">The Head</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Before any content renders, the browser reads the head: title, description, favicon, canonical link, and Open Graph tags. None of it is visible on the page itself, but all of it decides how the page shows up everywhere else.</p>
    <div class="panel__body card-grid card-grid--2">
      <div>
        <label for="headDemoTitle">Title tag</label>
        <input type="text" id="headDemoTitle" class="text-input" value="Webpage Anatomy: An Interactive Guide" maxlength="70" />
        <label for="headDemoDescription" style="margin-top:0.75rem; display:block;">Meta description</label>
        <textarea id="headDemoDescription" class="text-input" rows="3" maxlength="160">A bright, hands-on guide to the anatomy of a webpage, with live demonstrations you can inspect.</textarea>
        <p class="mono" style="font-size:0.75rem; opacity:.65; margin-top:0.5rem;">These start out matching this page's real tags. Edit them and watch the preview update.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">How this looks when shared</p>
        <div class="share-preview">
          <div class="share-preview__image" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="share-preview__body">
            <p class="share-preview__domain">webpageanatomy.com</p>
            <p class="share-preview__title" id="sharePreviewTitle">Webpage Anatomy: An Interactive Guide</p>
            <p class="share-preview__desc" id="sharePreviewDesc">A bright, hands-on guide to the anatomy of a webpage, with live demonstrations you can inspect.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="a11y-list" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <div class="a11y-list__item">
        <span class="a11y-list__tag">TITLE</span>
        <p>Shows in the browser tab and as the clickable headline in search results. Keep it under about 60 characters or Google truncates it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="a11y-list__item">
        <span class="a11y-list__tag">CANONICAL</span>
        <p>Tells search engines which URL is the real one when the same content is reachable at more than one address, so ranking doesn't split between duplicates.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="a11y-list__item">
        <span class="a11y-list__tag">OG</span>
        <p>Open Graph tags control the title, description, and image that Facebook, LinkedIn, and iMessage show when a link is pasted or shared.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="a11y-list__item">
        <span class="a11y-list__tag">FAVICON</span>
        <p>The small icon in the browser tab. It's a tiny detail, but a missing one makes a site look unfinished next to every other open tab.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Behind the scenes</p>
    <h2 id="privacy-heading">What the Eye Can't See</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Every page quietly collects more than what's on screen. Here's what that is, why it needs disclosing, and where the paperwork actually comes from.</p>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <div class="a11y-list">
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">TRACK</span>
          <p><strong>Cookies &amp; tracking.</strong> Small files or scripts that remember who a visitor is between visits: login sessions, shopping carts, analytics like Google Analytics, ad pixels like Meta's. First-party cookies come from the site itself; third-party ones come from someone else's script running on it, usually for advertising.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">PRIVACY</span>
          <p><strong>Privacy Policy.</strong> A page disclosing what data gets collected, why, and what a visitor can do about it. Legally required in most places (GDPR in the EU, CCPA in California) for almost any site running analytics or a contact form, not just stores.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">TERMS</span>
          <p><strong>Terms of Service.</strong> The rules for using the site: acceptable use, liability limits, who owns what. Not always legally required, but it's what protects the business if something goes wrong.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p><strong>Where to get one:</strong> free generators like Termly, GetTerms, and PrivacyPolicies.com produce a reasonable starting template for a small site. If the site handles payments, health data, or anything sensitive, a template stops being enough, and it's worth paying an actual lawyer. This is general orientation, not legal advice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="page-load" aria-labelledby="page-load-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Before you see anything</p>
    <h2 id="page-load-heading">How a Page Loads</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Typing a URL and seeing a finished page feels instant, but it is six steps happening in order. Press play and watch them happen one at a time.</p>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <div class="page-load-steps" id="pageLoadSteps">
        <div class="page-load-step" data-step="1"><span class="page-load-step__num">1</span><strong>DNS</strong><p>Look up which server the domain actually points to.</p></div>
        <div class="page-load-step" data-step="2"><span class="page-load-step__num">2</span><strong>Request</strong><p>Ask that server for the page over HTTPS.</p></div>
        <div class="page-load-step" data-step="3"><span class="page-load-step__num">3</span><strong>HTML</strong><p>Download the HTML and start building the DOM.</p></div>
        <div class="page-load-step" data-step="4"><span class="page-load-step__num">4</span><strong>CSS</strong><p>Download and apply styles before painting anything.</p></div>
        <div class="page-load-step" data-step="5"><span class="page-load-step__num">5</span><strong>Render</strong><p>Paint the styled page to the screen.</p></div>
        <div class="page-load-step" data-step="6"><span class="page-load-step__num">6</span><strong>JS</strong><p>Run JavaScript and wire up anything interactive.</p></div>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary" id="pageLoadPlay" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">Play the sequence</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="performance" aria-labelledby="performance-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Under the hood</p>
    <h2 id="performance-heading">Performance (And How to Prove It)</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">A beautiful page that takes eight seconds to load loses people before they see it. Here's what actually gets measured, in three numbers.</p>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <div class="card-grid" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
        <div class="sticker teal">
          <h3>LCP</h3>
          <p>Largest Contentful Paint: how fast the main content appears. Good is under 2.5 seconds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sticker coral">
          <h3>INP</h3>
          <p>Interaction to Next Paint: how fast the page responds to a click or tap. Good is under 200 milliseconds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sticker pink">
          <h3>CLS</h3>
          <p>Cumulative Layout Shift: how much content jumps around while loading. Good is under 0.1.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="panel__lede">These are Google's Core Web Vitals, and every tool below checks them for free.</p>
      <div class="a11y-list" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">PERF</span>
          <p><strong>Google PageSpeed Insights</strong> and <strong>WebPageTest.</strong> Paste in a URL, get real Core Web Vitals scores and a specific list of what's slow.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">A11Y</span>
          <p><strong>WAVE</strong> (by WebAIM) and <strong>axe DevTools.</strong> Browser extensions that scan a live page for contrast failures, missing alt text, and other accessibility issues.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">SEO</span>
          <p><strong>Google Search Console.</strong> Free, and it's how Google itself tells a site owner what it can and can't find or index.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">ALL 4</span>
          <p><strong>Lighthouse,</strong> built into Chrome DevTools (open DevTools, click the Lighthouse tab). One free report covering performance, accessibility, best practices, and SEO at once. Start here before reaching for anything else.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="images-media" aria-labelledby="images-media-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The heaviest part of most pages</p>
    <h2 id="images-media-heading">Images and Media</h2>
    <p class="panel__lede">Images are usually the biggest thing a browser downloads. The format, the file size, and whether the browser knows the dimensions in advance all matter.</p>
    <div class="panel__body">
      <div class="a11y-list">
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">SVG</span>
          <p>Best for logos, icons, and illustrations. Scales to any size with zero quality loss and is usually a tiny file.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">AVIF</span>
          <p>The newest, smallest photo format. Great compression, with slightly less universal browser support than WebP.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">WEBP</span>
          <p>The safe modern default for photos. Smaller than JPG at the same quality, and supported everywhere that matters now.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="a11y-list__item">
          <span class="a11y-list__tag">JPG</span>
          <p>The old universal fallback. Still fine, just larger than it needs to be for the same visual quality.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="panel__lede" style="margin-top:2rem;">The Performance section above mentions CLS, Cumulative Layout Shift. This is the single most common cause of it: an image with no reserved space. Click below to see it happen.</p>
    <div class="panel__body card-grid card-grid--2">
      <div>
        <p class="spacing-example__label bad">✗ No width/height set</p>
        <div class="image-shift-demo">
          <p>Some text above the image.</p>
          <div class="image-shift-demo__slot" id="imageShiftBadSlot"></div>
          <p>Some text below, about to get pushed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="spacing-example__label good">✓ Width/height reserved</p>
        <div class="image-shift-demo">
          <p>Some text above the image.</p>
          <div class="image-shift-demo__slot image-shift-demo__slot--reserved" id="imageShiftGoodSlot"></div>
          <p>Some text below, staying put.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn--primary" id="imageShiftPlay" style="margin-top:1rem;">Simulate a slow image load</button>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p><strong>Also worth knowing:</strong> <span class="mono">loading="lazy"</span> defers offscreen images until they're about to scroll into view, and <span class="mono">srcset</span> lets the browser pick a smaller image file for a smaller screen instead of downloading one giant image for everyone.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="parallax" aria-labelledby="parallax-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Scroll effects</p>
    <div class="panel__body parallax" data-parallax="true">
      <div class="parallax__inner">
        <p class="eyebrow">The depth illusion</p>
        <h2 id="parallax-heading">Parallax</h2>
        <p>Notice how this text sits still while the field behind it holds its position as you scroll? Disabled automatically if you've asked your system to reduce motion.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <p><strong>The Hot Sauce Rule:</strong> a little parallax goes a long way. Use it for one hero-scale moment, not every scroll.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="dictionary" aria-labelledby="dictionary-heading">
